  "transcript": "# Article 2 of the Protocol/Windsor Framework\n\n## Why Article 2 was included — and why the DUP opposes it\n> Gavin Robinson says Article 2 was unnecessary, politically motivated, and has caused harm to unionist confidence by altering Northern Ireland’s constitutional standing.\n\nChair: Gavin, please introduce yourself briefly for the record and make any opening remarks.\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: I'm Gavin Robinson, leader of the DUP and MP for Belfast, formerly on European scrutiny committees. I welcome this detailed scrutiny of Article 2 and the Windsor Framework.\nChair: We want to start by asking why Article 2 was negotiated and what its political significance is.\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: I don't believe Article 2 was necessary. It was negotiated to calm fears that I think were politically motivated and misplaced about rights being lost after Brexit.\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: Rights in Northern Ireland were already protected by domestic law and the Belfast agreement's commitments. Adding Article 2 created an impression of a special legal relationship with the EU that unionists never supported.\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: Politically, it backfired: meant to protect rights, it has weakened unionist confidence in Northern Ireland's place in the UK and in the consent principle of the Belfast agreement.\n\n\n## Impact on the Belfast agreement and sovereignty\n> Robinson argues Article 2 risks creating a 'rights border' and undermines the Belfast agreement's consent and constitutional settlement.\n\nLord Dodds of Duncairn: You say Article 2 breaches the Belfast agreement. Which parts specifically concern you?\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: The Belfast agreement rests on Northern Ireland's place in the UK unless people consent otherwise. Article 2, by elevating protections tied to EU standards and being applied without unionist consent, undermines that consent and constitutional clarity.\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: It has damaged political stability here; for unionists it looks like decisions about our rights may be taken by others outside the UK framework.\nChair: You accept UK parliamentary sovereignty, but want Parliament to respect the Belfast agreement as an international treaty that shaped the settlement?\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: Yes. I don't claim Northern Ireland sovereignty. I expect the UK Parliament to consider the Belfast agreement—an international treaty—when making changes that affect Northern Ireland's constitutional position.\n\n\n## The Dillon judgment and the search for legal clarity\n> Dillon narrowed some interpretations of Article 2, but Robinson says it didn't remove serious uncertainty; he wants Parliament and government to state their interpretation clearly, possibly by legislation.\n\nBaroness Foster of Aghadrumsee: Victims' groups point out some rights weren't in place in 2020—so 'no diminution' is unclear. How do you see the Dillon judgment?\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: Dillon helped by narrowing vague claims, but it's partial. It tackled some expansionist readings, yet left many questions about what counts as 'no diminution' and which rights are in scope.\nBaroness Ludford: How should we get more clarity—another government explainer or something stronger?\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: An explanatory note won't be enough. Courts will keep being asked to interpret Article 2. I think the Government should make a clear declaratory statement in Parliament, and if needed legislate, to limit Article 2's scope.\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: Government post-scripts won't bind judges; Parliament needs to be clearer about intention so courts and departments know how to apply the law.\nBaroness Foster of Aghadrumsee: Some groups say Dillon doesn't help victims who never had certain rights—how does that fit?\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: There are different legal and moral definitions of 'victim' for different processes. We mustn't assume one legal definition covers every claim; many of these issues are political as well as legal.\n\n\n## Everyday effects, dynamic alignment and political choices\n> Robinson described practical problems from the Framework, warned a 'reset' that aligns Great Britain to the EU could be temporary, and said limited engagement with ministers leaves uncertainty.\n\nBaroness Sanderson of Welton: Do people in Northern Ireland understand Article 2? Are fears real even if people can't name the clause?\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: Many ordinary people feel they're treated differently: goods not available, firms filling forms to ship within the UK, public items not deliverable to Northern Ireland. They may not cite Article 2, but they feel unequal treatment.\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: This isn't just theoretical: product-safety rules have made posting items across the UK harder—for customers and small businesses in Northern Ireland.\nBaroness Ritchie of Downpatrick: How can rights for all be protected given differing views here?\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: The Belfast agreement can accommodate competing aspirations, but the government must not accept measures that, in practice, reduce the rights or status of unionists within the UK without proper consent.\nLord Elliott of Ballinamallard: Post-Dillon, how should government and the Executive meet rights obligations and avoid creating new injustices?\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: The UK must assert that its courts interpret UK law and Parliament can legislate uniformly. Right now there's confusion about whether Westminster can act without external constraints: that must be settled clearly.\nLord Dodds of Duncairn: About a 'reset' that aligns Great Britain: how reliable is that as a solution for Northern Ireland?\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: A reset that makes Great Britain match EU rules might relieve some friction, but it's fragile: a future UK government could reverse it, leaving Northern Ireland back where it is now—and it also risks sowing resentment in other UK nations forced to follow.\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: Also, 'separate but equal' arrangements for Northern Ireland are unacceptable—the analogy to outdated US segregation shows why.\nChair: Are Northern Ireland parties being consulted on the reset?\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: My contact with ministers has been limited: a monthly catch-up with the relevant minister previously, some discussions with the Windsor Framework team, but no detailed engagement with the Secretary of State on these issues since his appointment.\nChair: If this committee concluded Article 2 was redundant and should be repealed, how would people here react?\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: Unionists would welcome it enthusiastically. But I expect other parties to resist; they may not engage with the committee's work unless it suits them politically.\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: Ultimately, the Government must be clearer, act through Parliament if necessary, and restore confidence that Northern Ireland's place in the UK is not being eroded by external interpretation of rights.\nChair: Thank you. We must close now; you'll receive the transcript for correction.\nRt Hon Gavin Robinson MP: Thank you. I appreciate the scrutiny and hope it leads to clearer government action.\n",
